WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.022
<v Examiner>Exam case 6, continued.

2
00:00:03.022 --> 00:00:06.807
<v Examiner>Glucagon, the blood sugar day, and the tired pancreas.

3
00:00:07.257 --> 00:00:10.984
These five aren't starred, but they sit inside the yellow case,

4
00:00:10.984 --> 00:00:15.479
and her deck answers every one of them, on slides thirty-one to thirty-four.

5
00:00:15.479 --> 00:00:18.969
Her helpline says an unstarred question can still be asked.

6
00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:20.172
<v Examiner>Three marks.

7
00:00:20.172 --> 00:00:23.750
<v Examiner>What other molecules also influence blood glucose levels?

8
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.587
Glucagon, and it raises blood glucose three ways.

9
00:00:27.587 --> 00:00:31.457
It stimulates the breakdown of glycogen: glycogenolysis.

10
00:00:31.457 --> 00:00:34.705
It stimulates the breakdown of fats: lipolysis.

11
00:00:34.705 --> 00:00:40.925
And it stimulates the liver to make new glucose from non-glucose sources: gluconeogenesis.

12
00:00:41.375 --> 00:00:42.083
<v Examiner>Four marks.

13
00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:45.235
<v Examiner>Insulin is designed to only be high after a meal,

14
00:00:45.235 --> 00:00:49.094
<v Examiner>so in normal function it follows a pattern like the diagram.

15
00:00:49.094 --> 00:00:53.017
<v Examiner>Can you describe the normal blood glucose level over the day?

16
00:00:53.017 --> 00:00:54.239
<v Examiner>What about insulin?

17
00:00:54.689 --> 00:00:59.876
Blood glucose changes over the day with your meals, especially carbs and sugar:

18
00:00:59.876 --> 00:01:01.517
it rises after each meal.

19
00:01:01.517 --> 00:01:04.735
But there's always a background level of insulin.

20
00:01:04.735 --> 00:01:06.376
Insulin is never at zero.

21
00:01:06.376 --> 00:01:11.103
Blood glucose is never at zero either: it stays within the normal range.

22
00:01:11.103 --> 00:01:15.633
And her two words for insulin: basal insulin is the background level,

23
00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:19.573
and bolus insulin is what's released, or given, after meals.

24
00:01:21.023 --> 00:01:21.589
<v Examiner>One mark.

25
00:01:21.589 --> 00:01:27.188
<v Examiner>In normal blood sugar regulation, does the blood glucose level go above the normal range?

26
00:01:27.638 --> 00:01:27.803
No.

27
00:01:27.803 --> 00:01:29.835
It shouldn't exceed the normal range.

28
00:01:30.285 --> 00:01:30.804
<v Examiner>Two marks.

29
00:01:30.804 --> 00:01:31.998
<v Examiner>What happens overnight?

30
00:01:31.998 --> 00:01:34.125
<v Examiner>And what could that mean for the morning?

31
00:01:34.575 --> 00:01:37.205
Blood glucose drops to its lowest overnight.

32
00:01:37.205 --> 00:01:40.492
And breakfast is breaking the fast: you wake up hungry,

33
00:01:40.492 --> 00:01:43.002
because your body has run out of glycogen.

34
00:01:44.452 --> 00:01:45.142
<v Examiner>Three marks.

35
00:01:45.142 --> 00:01:48.479
<v Examiner>When the body's cells are resistant to the insulin signal,

36
00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:51.701
<v Examiner>the pancreas tries to compensate by making more insulin.

37
00:01:51.701 --> 00:01:55.614
<v Examiner>All that overwork can damage it, so now it struggles to make enough.

38
00:01:55.614 --> 00:01:59.641
<v Examiner>What would happen in the body now, and how might we need to intervene?

39
00:02:00.091 --> 00:02:04.121
Pancreatic fatigue: damage to the pancreas from overworking.

40
00:02:04.121 --> 00:02:08.487
Then low insulin levels, so blood glucose regulation is impaired.

41
00:02:08.487 --> 00:02:13.323
That's type 2 diabetes mellitus, and the person might end up on insulin.

42
00:02:13.323 --> 00:02:15.473
Which is exactly where Larry is.

43
00:02:15.923 --> 00:02:16.503
<v Examiner>Once more.

44
00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:17.779
<v Examiner>Glucagon, and the day.

45
00:02:20.229 --> 00:02:24.832
Glucagon raises blood glucose three ways: glycogenolysis, lipolysis,

46
00:02:24.832 --> 00:02:27.066
and gluconeogenesis in the liver.

47
00:02:27.066 --> 00:02:30.248
Over the day, blood glucose follows your meals,

48
00:02:30.248 --> 00:02:36.274
but it never hits zero and stays in the normal range, and insulin never hits zero either.

49
00:02:36.274 --> 00:02:40.674
Basal insulin is the background; bolus insulin comes after meals.

50
00:02:40.674 --> 00:02:44.601
Normally, blood glucose never goes above the normal range.

51
00:02:44.601 --> 00:02:48.121
It's lowest overnight, so breakfast breaks the fast.

52
00:02:48.121 --> 00:02:54.147
Insulin resistance overworks the pancreas until it fatigues and makes too little insulin:

53
00:02:54.147 --> 00:02:57.464
type 2 diabetes, and possibly insulin injections.
